@* Brand mark: one rounded tile split corner-to-corner by an S-curve into two fields, each carrying a dot in the
   other's tone — the pair sharing one account. Replaced the two-heads-under-a-beam mark, which read as two
   people and dated the app to a couples-only product.

   Why an S and not a single bow: the S crosses dead centre whatever its depth, so both fields stay equal and
   both dots stay equally clear of the divide. A one-way bow moves the split toward one dot and away from the
   other, so the curve amount and the dot positions stop being independent.

   Field colours come from CSS custom properties so the mark tracks the theme (dark brightens the light half to
   mint); the inline defaults mean a host that sets neither still renders the original green. The two dot tones
   are fixed — they are chosen for contrast against their own field, not for the theme.

   ⚠ The dots carry classes. Spinner.razor.css animates them, and the previous mark's loader selected a bare
   `circle`, so deleting the heads would have silently orphaned the animation with no error. Do not remove
   .tt-dot-a / .tt-dot-b without updating the spinner. *@
<svg class="tandem-logo @(Pro ? "is-pro" : "")" viewBox="0 0 64 64" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="@(Pro ? "TandemTab Pro" : "TandemTab")">
    <defs>
        @* Per-instance id: several TandemLogos can share a page (app bar + a loader), and SVG ids are
           document-global, so a fixed id would be duplicate markup. *@
        <clipPath id="@_clipId"><rect width="64" height="64" rx="17" /></clipPath>
    </defs>
    <g clip-path="url(#@_clipId)">
        <rect width="64" height="64" fill="var(--tt-logo-2, #13a06e)" />
        @* The split is drawn well past the tile on both ends so the shape stays valid under any transform; the
           clip does the trimming. Its endpoints are the tile's own corners, which sit inside the corner radius
           and therefore cannot be expressed as a closed path along the tile outline — hence the clip. *@
        <path d="M96 -32 L64 0 C62.7 20 52 30.7 32 32 C12 33.3 1.3 44 0 64 L-32 96 L-32 -32 Z"
              fill="var(--tt-logo-1, #34d399)" />
        <circle class="tt-dot-a" cx="20" cy="20" r="8" fill="#0b6b4a" />
        <circle class="tt-dot-b" cx="44" cy="44" r="8" fill="#a7f3d0" />
    </g>
    @if (Pro)
    {
        @* Pro flourish: a small gold crown worn at a tilt by the upper dot. Deliberately perched rather than
           centred — a symmetrical crown reads as a logo change, whereas a jaunty one reads as a decoration on
           the same mark, which is what a plan badge should be. It sits outside the clip so it can overlap the
           tile edge, and on the UPPER-LEFT dot so the tilt leans into the mark rather than away from it. *@
        @* scale 1.333 = 64/48: the crown asset was drawn against the mark laid out in a 48-unit tile, and this
           tile is full-bleed 64. Scaling it by anything else leaves the crown hovering off its dot instead of
           resting across it. *@
        <g transform="rotate(-18 20 12.7) translate(10.7 4) scale(1.333)">
            <path d="M0 7 L0 1.6 L3.5 4.4 L7 0 L10.5 4.4 L14 1.6 L14 7 Z" fill="#f6c945" />
            <rect x="-0.4" y="6.6" width="14.8" height="2.4" rx="1.2" fill="#e0a92c" />
        </g>
    }
</svg>

@code {
    /// <summary>Renders the Pro flourish. Purely decorative — entitlement is decided server-side, so a client
    /// that set this by hand would gain a crown and nothing else.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Pro { get; set; }

    private readonly string _clipId = $"tt-mark-{Guid.NewGuid():N}"[..16];
}
